From f73ddcf64d34de03318fde2a6c8d25a43c661dc0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: James Reilly <jreilly1821@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 21:28:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 7/8] installer: Ask for a disk passphrase when there is no TPM

Without a TPM 2.0 the installed root cannot be bound to one, and making
the recovery key the only key means typing a 64-character key at every
boot. Add a disk-passphrase page, built only when no TPM 2.0 is present,
between the installer and the recovery key page: the user picks a
passphrase (entered twice), which is enrolled through
io.systemd.CryptEnroll (mechanism password, unlocked with the empty
password SysInstall enrolled). The recovery key page then wipes the
other password slots but keeps that one, so the recovery key is a second
key rather than the only one.

The definitions must not ask for tpm2 on such machines (repart refuses
to encrypt with tpm2 when there is no TPM); distributions that write
their definitions at boot can pick Encrypt=key-file there.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus via GitHub Copilot
Co-authored-by: Copilot <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
Signed-off-by: James Reilly <jreilly1821@gmail.com>
